## Why This Is Asked

Explores a novel variation of LPS with deletions, forcing a 3D DP and careful reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP: i, j bounds plus remaining deletions t.
- Transitions handle match, deletion of either end, and base cases.
- Reconstruction by tracing choices from dp[0][n-1][k].
- Space optimization via rolling t or two 2D layers.

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxLPSWithDeletions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][i][0] = 1;
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (s[i] === s[j]) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], (i+1<=j-1? dp[i+1][j-1][t] : 0) + 2);
        }
        if (t > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i+1][j][t-1], dp[i][j-1][t-1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0][n-1][k] || 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(nk) space while preserving O(n^2 k) time?
- How does the solution change if deletions must be exactly k?

## Why This Is Asked
Models practical scheduling under capacity with a convex (squared) day cost, exercising 2D DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and days
- Transition constrained by daily capacity D
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Backtracking via parent pointers to recover daily blocks
- Complexity: O(n^2 m) time, O(n m) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSchedule(A, m, D) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(m + 1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(m + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let d = 1; d <= m; d++) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const sum = pref[i] - pref[j];
        if (sum <= D && dp[j][d - 1] !== INF) {
          const cost = dp[j][d - 1] + sum * sum;
          if (cost < dp[i][d]) {
            dp[i][d] = cost;
            par[i][d] = j;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  if (dp[n][m] === INF) return { cost: -1, blocks: [] };
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, d = m;
  while (d > 0) {
    const j = par[i][d];
    blocks.push([j + 1, i]);
    i = j;
    d--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { cost: dp[n][m], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to near O(n m) if D is small or sums have tighter bounds?
- How would you present the actual day blocks to the user, including start/end indices and sums?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a beginner-friendly DP pattern with non-adjacent selections and reconstruction. It also shows handling multiple dimensions (i and t) and a simple heuristic constraint (no two boosts on consecutive days).

## Key Concepts
- DP with two states per index for constrained selections
- Reconstruction of chosen days
- Time/space: O(nk); note can optimize to O(k) space with rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxWorkload(workload, k) {
  const n = workload.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const noBoost = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const boost = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  noBoost[0][0] = workload[0];
  if (k >= 1) boost[0][1] = 2 * workload[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      noBoost[i][t] = Math.max(noBoost[i-1][t], boost[i-1][t]) + workload[i];
    }
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      if (noBoost[i-1][t-1] > NEG) boost[i][t] = noBoost[i-1][t-1] + 2*workload[i];
    }
  }
  let best = NEG, bestT = 0, bestS = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    if (noBoost[n-1][t] > best) { best = noBoost[n-1][t]; bestT = t; bestS = 0; }
    if (boost[n-1][t] > best) { best = boost[n-1][t]; bestT = t; bestS = 1; }
  }
  // reconstruction would backtrack using saved states (omitted for brevity)
  return { maxValue: best, boostedIndices: [] };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Scheduling perspective: minimize peak workload across days; the DP is a straightforward partition DP with a range max cost. It tests understanding of maintaining running max within inner loop and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes
- Range max within an inner loop
- Path reconstruction via cut[]
- Edge cases: empty input, large n, identical durations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(L){
  const n=L.length;
  const dp=new Array(n+1).fill(Infinity);
  const cut=new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    let curMax=0;
    for(let k=1;k<=i;k++){
      curMax = Math.max(curMax, L[i-k]);
      const val = dp[i-k] + curMax;
      if(val < dp[i]){ dp[i] = val; cut[i] = k; }
    }
  }
  const days=[];
  let i=n;
  while(i>0){
    const k=cut[i];
    days.push(L.slice(i-k, i));
    i -= k;
  }
  days.reverse();
  return {cost: dp[n], days};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a 2D grid of tasks with row/day semantics?
- Can you optimize the inner loop to O(n) using a data structure for range max?

## Why This Is Asked
This gauges ability to cast a line-wrapping task into a clear 1D DP with a non-linear line cost and a reconstruction path. It tests handling of last-line quirks and feasibility checks.

## Key Concepts
- 1D DP over word indices; dp[n] = 0 as base.
- Prefix sums to compute line width in O(1) per extension.
- Cost function uses squared unused space; last line excluded.
- Reconstruct solution via next indices.
- Time: O(n^2); Space: O(n).

## Code Example
```javascript
function minWrap(words, W) {
  const n = words.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i + 1] = pref[i] + words[i];
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(Infinity);
  const nxt = new Array(n).fill(-1);
  dp[n] = 0;
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      const width = (pref[j + 1] - pref[i]) + (j - i);
      if (width <= W) {
        const cost = (W - width) * (W - width) + dp[j + 1];
        if (cost < dp[i]) { dp[i] = cost; nxt[i] = j + 1; }
      } else break;
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return { minPenalty: dp[0], next: nxt };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify to penalize the last line as well?
- How would you incorporate tie-breaking to prefer fewer lines?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to generalize DP with an extra resource dimension and reconstruction, a common pattern in grid path problems with constraints. It also probes edge-case handling when i or j is at 0 and when t=0. 

## Key Concepts
- DP with an extra dimension for resource usage (K diagonals)
- 3-way transitions and path reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPath(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const INF = 1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(K+1).fill(INF)));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(K+1).fill(null)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0 && t === 0) continue;
        let best = INF, pick = null;
        if (i > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t] < best) { best = dp[i-1][j][t]; pick = [i-1, j, t]; }
        if (j > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t] < best) { best = dp[i][j-1][t]; pick = [i, j-1, t]; }
        if (i > 0 && j > 0 && t > 0 && dp[i-1][j-1][t-1] < best) { best = dp[i-1][j-1][t-1]; pick = [i-1, j-1, t-1]; }
        if (best < INF) {
          dp[i][j][t] = best + grid[i][j];
          if (pick) parent[i][j][t] = pick;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // find best end state
  let bestCost = INF, endT = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
    if (dp[n-1][m-1][t] < bestCost) { bestCost = dp[n-1][m-1][t]; endT = t; }
  }
  // reconstruct path
  const path = [];
  let i = n-1, j = m-1, t = endT;
  while (i !== 0 || j !== 0 || t !== 0) {
    path.push([i, j]);
    const p = parent[i][j][t];
    if (!p) break;
    [i, j, t] = p;
  }
  path.push([0,0]);
  path.reverse();
  return { cost: bestCost, path };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reduce space to O(m K) using rolling arrays?
- How would you handle negative costs or unreachable endpoints?

## Why This Problem Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming on partitioning with a non-linear cost function (block maximum). It extends beyond standard max-sum variants and evaluates careful indexing, prefix handling, and solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- **Contiguous partition DP**: State dp[i][t] represents the minimum cost for the prefix of length i using exactly t blocks
- **Transition**: Computes the running maximum for the last block while finding the optimal split point
- **Reconstruction**: Backtracking through the DP table to determine block endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumOfBlockMax(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const split = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i));
  }

  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      let maxVal = 0;
      for (let j = i - 1; j >= t - 1; j--) {
        maxVal = Math.max(maxVal, A[j]);
        const cost = dp[j][t - 1] + maxVal;
        if (cost < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = cost;
          split[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = split[i][t];
    blocks.unshift({ start: j + 1, end: i });
    i = j;
    t--;
  }

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a new DP variant: maximizing the sum of non-additive subarray values (bitwise OR) while enforcing exactly k non-overlapping blocks. It also probes reconstruction logic for end-to-start blocks.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitioning into k non-overlapping blocks
- Non-additive block value via OR, requiring on-the-fly accumulation
- Backpointers for exact block endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKOrSubarrays(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prv=Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      let curr=0;
      for(let j=i;j>=0;j--){
        curr |= A[j];
        let left = dp[j][t-1];
        if(left>-Infinity){
          const cand = left + curr;
          if(cand>dp[i+1][t]){ dp[i+1][t]=cand; prv[i+1][t]=j; }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return {value: dp[n][k], endAt: prv};
}
```

